  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Showery and breezy today.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nSurface/upper lows vertically stack across the Maritimes today \nslowing the movement of the system. A disturbance moving around\nthe Maritimes system will rotate across the region today. \nIsolated/scattered showers will persist across the region \ntoday, then diminish tonight. The Maritimes system slowly moves\neast Sunday. Dependent on how rapidly the Maritimes system \nmoves, isolated/scattered showers wrapping around the system\ncould persist across eastern portions of the forecast area \nearly Sunday. The better chances would occur along the \nMaine/New Brunswick border. The pressure gradient between the \nMaritimes low and high pressure to the west will help support \nbreezy conditions today. North/northeast winds will gust up to \n20 to 25 mph today.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Rain chances remain limited through midweek,\naccompanied by near to below normal temperatures. The next best\nchance for precipitation arrives Wednesday night into Thursday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nThe low pressure system shift to the NE and exits over the\nMaritimes. A large upper level ridge will progress across the \nregion. Surface high pressure should keep clouds away Sunday\nnight and Monday. This should result in temps in the low 40s \nSunday night and upper 70s on Monday. Shortwave energy should \ncross the region on Tuesday bringing cloud cover that will \nreduce high temps and increase low temps. Dry conditions with \nbelow normal low temps will continue to Wednesday night. \nEnsembles show increasing confidence in a large closed low \npressure system moving across Canada. However, significant \nuncertain on the track and timing of the low exists across all \ndeterministic models.",
